{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2237 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "219decdf6ab831a1ffc187b3fd17da2b91034e6577d4daacde3798ebcd0d8707" :edb/db-before-t 2236 :edb/db-after-t 2237 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535549 50 #inst "2026-10-14T11:11:06.533Z" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054500 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac {2}{\\sqrt{3} - 1}$." 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186054500 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac {2}{\\sqrt{3} - 1}.$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054500 1074 "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{3} + 1$.\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{3} + 1$, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {2}{\\sqrt{3} - 1} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {2}{\\sqrt{3} - 1} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\sqrt{3} + 1} {\\sqrt{3} + 1} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {2\\left(\\sqrt 3 + 1\\right)} {\\left(\\sqrt{3}\\right)^2 - 1^2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {2\\left(\\sqrt 3 + 1\\right)} {3 - 1} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {2\\left(\\sqrt 3 + 1\\right)} {2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\sqrt 3 + 1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186054500 1074 "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{3} + 1.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{3} + 1,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {2}{\\sqrt{3} - 1} &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {2}{\\sqrt{3} - 1} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\sqrt{3} + 1} {\\sqrt{3} + 1} &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {2\\left(\\sqrt 3 + 1\\right)} {\\left(\\sqrt{3}\\right)^2 - 1^2} &= \\[5pt]\t\n\t\t\\dfrac {2\\left(\\sqrt 3 + 1\\right)} {3 - 1} &= \\[5pt]\t\t\n\t\t\\dfrac {2\\left(\\sqrt 3 + 1\\right)} {2} &= \\[5pt]\t\n\t\t\\sqrt 3 + 1 &\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054501 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac{6}{\\sqrt{10} - \\sqrt{6}}$." 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186054501 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac{6}{\\sqrt{10} - \\sqrt{6}}.$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054501 1074 "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{10} + \\sqrt{6}$.\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{10} + \\sqrt{6}$, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10} - \\sqrt{6}} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10} - \\sqrt{6}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10} + \\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{10} + \\sqrt{6}} &=  \\\\\n(6(\\sqrt{10} + \\sqrt{6}))/((\\sqrt{10})^{2} - (\\sqrt{6})^{2}) &=  \\\\\n(6(\\sqrt{10} + \\sqrt{6}))/(10 - 6) &=  \\\\\n(6(\\sqrt{10} + \\sqrt{6}))/(4) &=  \\\\\n&= (3(\\sqrt{10} + \\sqrt{6}))/(2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186054501 1074 "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{10} + \\sqrt{6}.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{10} + \\sqrt{6},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{6}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6(\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6})}{(\\sqrt{10})^{2}-(\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6(\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6})}{10-6} & = \\\\ \\frac{6(\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6})}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6})}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054502 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac{2 - \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2} + 1}$." 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186054502 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac{2 - \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2} + 1}.$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054502 1074 "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{2} - 1$.\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{2} - 1$, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2 - \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2} + 1} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2 - \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2} + 1} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2} - 1}{\\sqrt{2} - 1} &=  \\\\\n((2 - \\sqrt{2})(\\sqrt{2} - 1))/((\\sqrt{2} + 1)(\\sqrt{2} - 1)) &=  \\\\\n(2\\sqrt{2} - 2 - (\\sqrt{2})^{2} + \\sqrt{2})/((\\sqrt{2})^{2} - (1)^{2}) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2\\sqrt{2} - 2 - 2 + \\sqrt{2}}{2 - 1} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2\\sqrt{2} - 4 + \\sqrt{2}}{1} &=  \\\\\n&= 3\\sqrt{2} - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186054502 1074 "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{2} - 1.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{2} - 1,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}+1} & = \\\\ \\frac{2-\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}-1}{\\sqrt{2}-1} & = \\\\ \\frac{(2-\\sqrt{2})(\\sqrt{2}-1)}{(\\sqrt{2}+1)(\\sqrt{2}-1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-2-(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+\\sqrt{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}-(1)^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-2-2+\\sqrt{2}}{2-1} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-4+\\sqrt{2}}{1} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054525 1071 "What is the value of $4\\textrm{i}^3$?" 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186054525 1071 "What is the value of $4\\textrm{i}^3?$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054525 1074 "Let's recall the first few powers of $\\textrm{i}$, where\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i} = \\sqrt{-1}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\textrm{i}^{0} &= 1 \\\\\n\\textrm{i}^{1} &= \\textrm{i} \\\\\n\\textrm{i}^{2} &= -1 \\\\\n\\textrm{i}^{3} &= \\textrm{i}^{2} \\cdot \\textrm{i} = (-1) \\cdot \\textrm{i} =-\\textrm{i} \\\\\n\\textrm{i}^{4} &= \\textrm{i}^{2} \\cdot \\textrm{i}^{2} = (-1) \\cdot (-1) = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4\\textrm{i}^{3} &= 4 \\cdot (-\\textrm{i}) =-4\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186054525 1074 "Let's recall the first few powers of $\\textrm{i},$ where $\\textrm{i} = \\sqrt{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{i}}^{0} & =1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{1} & =\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{3} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot \\text{i}=(-1)\\cdot \\text{i}=-\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{4} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot {\\text{i}}^{2}=(-1)\\cdot (-1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\text{i}}^{3} & =4\\cdot (-\\text{i})=-4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054526 1071 "Simplify $\\textrm{i}^{15}$." 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186054526 1071 "Simplify $\\textrm{i}^{15}.$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054526 1074 "To find the value of $\\textrm{i}$ to some positive integer power $n$, we divide $n$ by $4$ and raise $\\textrm{i}$ to the remainder.\n\nIn our case, we have $n=15$. Dividing the power by $4$, we get\n\n$$\n15\\div 4 = 3\\,\\textrm{R} \\color{blue}{3}\n$$\n\nSo the remainder is $\\color{blue}3$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^{15} = \\textrm{i}^{\\color{blue}{3}} = -\\textrm{i}\n$$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186054526 1074 "To find the value of $\\textrm{i}$ to some positive integer power $n$, we divide $n$ by $4$ and raise $\\textrm{i}$ to the remainder.\n\nIn our case, we have $n=15.$ Dividing the power by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n15\\div 4 = 3\\,\\textrm{R} \\color{blue}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo the remainder is $\\color{blue}3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^{15} = \\textrm{i}^{\\color{blue}{3}} = -\\textrm{i}.\n$$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054527 1071 "If $a = \\textrm{i}^9$ and $b = \\textrm{i}^{7}$, then $ab =$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186054527 1071 "If $a = \\textrm{i}^9$ and $b = \\textrm{i}^{7},$ then $ab =$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054527 1074 "Using the addition law for exponents, we have\n\n$$\nab =\\textrm{i}^9\\cdot\\textrm{i}^7 = \\textrm{i}^{9+7} = \\textrm{i}^{16}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\textrm{i}$ raised to some positive integer power $n$, we divide $n$ by $4$ and raise $\\textrm{i}$ to the remainder.\n\nIn our case, we have $n=16$. Dividing the power by $4$, we get\n\n$$\n16\\div 4 = 4\\,\\textrm{R} \\color{blue}{0}\n$$\n\nSo the remainder is $\\color{blue}0$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^{16} = \\textrm{i}^{\\color{blue}{0}} =1\n$$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186054527 1074 "Using the addition law for exponents, we have\n\n$$\nab =\\textrm{i}^9\\cdot\\textrm{i}^7 = \\textrm{i}^{9+7} = \\textrm{i}^{16}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\textrm{i}$ raised to some positive integer power $n,$ we divide $n$ by $4$ and raise $\\textrm{i}$ to the remainder.\n\nIn our case, we have $n=16.$ Dividing the power by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n16\\div 4 = 4\\,\\textrm{R} \\color{blue}{0}.\n$$\n\nSo the remainder is $\\color{blue}0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^{16} = \\textrm{i}^{\\color{blue}{0}} =1.\n$$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054528 1071 "Find the value of $\\textrm{i}^{-2}$." 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186054528 1071 "Find the value of $\\textrm{i}^{-2}.$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054528 1074 "First, using the laws of exponents, we can write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^{-2}=\\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{i}^2}\n$$\n\nWe now recall that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^2 = -1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{i}^2} = \\dfrac{1}{(-1)} = -1\n$$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186054528 1074 "First, using the laws of exponents, we can write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^{-2}=\\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{i}^2}.\n$$\n\nWe now recall that $\\textrm{i}^2 = -1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{i}^2} = \\dfrac{1}{(-1)} = -1.\n$$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054554 1071 "Solve $f(x)=0$, where $f(x) = \\dfrac{x+1}{x^2-x-2}$." 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186054554 1071 "Solve $f(x)=0,$ where $f(x) = \\dfrac{x+1}{x^2-x-2}.$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054554 1074 "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{x + 1}{x^{2} - x - 2} \\\\\n&= (x + 1)/((x + 1)(x - 2)) \\\\\n&= ((x + 1))/((x + 1)(x - 2)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x - 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the numerator equal to zero, we get $1=0$, which is a false statement. Therefore,\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\nhas no solution." 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186054554 1074 "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}-x-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+1}{(x+1)(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x+1)}}{\\cancel{(x+1)}(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the numerator equal to zero, we get $1=0,$ which is a false statement. Therefore, $f(x)=0$ has no solution." 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054555 1071 "Solve $f(x)=0$, where $f(x) = \\dfrac{x^2-4x}{x^2+5x+6}$." 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186054555 1071 "Solve $f(x)=0,$ where $f(x) = \\dfrac{x^2-4x}{x^2+5x+6}.$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054555 1074 "First, we factor $f(x)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{x^{2} - 4x}{x^{2} + 5x + 6} \\\\\n&= (x(x - 4))/((x + 2)(x + 3))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the numerator equal to zero and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(x - 4) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\nare $x=0$ and $x=4$." 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186054555 1074 "First, we factor $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-4x}{{x}^{2}+5x+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(x-4)}{(x+2)(x+3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the numerator equal to zero and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $f(x)=0$ are $x=0$ and $x=4.$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054556 1071 "Solve $f(x)=0$, where $f(x) = \\dfrac{x^3-2x^2-9x+18}{x^2-x-2}$." 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186054556 1071 "Solve $f(x)=0$, where $f(x) = \\dfrac{x^3-2x^2-9x+18}{x^2-x-2}.$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054556 1074 "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{x^{3} - 2x^{2} - 9x + 18}{x^{2} - x - 2} \\\\\n&= (x^{2}(x - 2) - 9(x - 2))/((x - 2)(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= ((x - 2)(x^{2} - 9))/((x - 2)(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= ((x - 2)(x - 3)(x + 3))/((x - 2)(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= ((x - 2)(x - 3)(x + 3))/((x - 2)(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= ((x - 3)(x + 3))/(x + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the numerator equal to zero and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n(x-3)(x+3) = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x = \\pm3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\nare $x=3$ and $x=-3$." 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186054556 1074 "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-9x+18}{{x}^{2}-x-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}(x-2)-9(x-2)}{(x-2)(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)({x}^{2}-9)}{(x-2)(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)(x-3)(x+3)}{(x-2)(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x-3)(x+3)}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the numerator equal to zero and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n(x-3)(x+3) = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x = \\pm3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $f(x)=0$ are $x=3$ and $x=-3.$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054566 1071 "Solve $f(x)=0$, where $f(x) = \\dfrac{2x + 1}{x^2 + x - 6}$." 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186054566 1071 "Solve $f(x)=0,$ where $f(x) = \\dfrac{2x + 1}{x^2 + x - 6}.$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054566 1074 "First, we factor $f(x)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{2x + 1}{x^{2} + x - 6} \\\\\n&= (2x + 1)/((x + 3)(x - 2))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the numerator equal to zero and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n2x &= -1 \\\\\nx &= -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186054566 1074 "First, we factor $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+1}{(x+3)(x-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the numerator equal to zero and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to $f(x)=0$ is $x=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054614 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515562 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054615 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515577 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054615 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515613 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054616 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515584 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054616 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515616 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054617 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515533 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054617 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515535 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054617 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515618 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054670 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515566 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054670 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515578 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054671 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515589 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054736 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515596 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054736 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515604 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186054737 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515521 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [175921