nsists of two terms and includes one or more radicals, we rationalize it by multiplying both the numerator and the denominator by the **conjugate** of the denominator.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{1+\\sqrt{2}}\n$$\n\nTo find the conjugate of the denominator $1+\\sqrt{2}$, we *flip the sign* between the two terms. So, the conjugate of the denominator is\n\n$$\n1\\,{\\color{blue}{-}}\\,\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we start by multiplying both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $1-\\sqrt{2}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{1 + \\sqrt{2}} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{1 + \\sqrt{2}} \\cdot \\frac{1 - \\sqrt{2}}{1 - \\sqrt{2}} &=  \\\\\n(1 \\cdot (1 - \\sqrt{2}))/((1 + \\sqrt{2})(1 - \\sqrt{2})) &=  \\\\\n(1 - \\sqrt{2})/((1 + \\sqrt{2})(1 - \\sqrt{2})) &= \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n(a+b)(a-b) = a^2 - b^2\n$$\n\nApplying the difference of squares formula with\n\n$$\na=1, b=\\sqrt 2\n$$\n\nto simplify the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1 - \\sqrt{2})/((1 + \\sqrt{2})(1 - \\sqrt{2})) &=  \\\\\n(1 - \\sqrt{2})/(1^{2} - (\\sqrt{2})^{2}) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1 - \\sqrt{2}}{1 - 2} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1 - \\sqrt{2}}{-1} &=  \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186055933 1064 "When simplifying numerical expressions, it is generally preferable to avoid having radicals in the denominator. In cases where we wish to remove a radical from a denominator, we must **rationalize the denominator**.\n\nIf the denominator of an expression consists of two terms and includes one or more radicals, we rationalize it by multiplying both the numerator and the denominator by the **conjugate** of the denominator. \n\nTo illustrate, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{1+\\sqrt{2}}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the conjugate of the denominator $1+\\sqrt{2},$ we *flip the sign* between the two terms. So, the conjugate of the denominator is \n\n$$\n1\\,{\\color{blue}{-}}\\,\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we start by multiplying both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $1-\\sqrt{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1+\\sqrt{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1-\\sqrt{2}}{1-\\sqrt{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\cdot (1-\\sqrt{2})}{(1+\\sqrt{2})(1-\\sqrt{2})} &  \\\\ \\frac{1-\\sqrt{2}}{(1+\\sqrt{2})(1-\\sqrt{2})} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n(a+b)(a-b) = a^2 - b^2\n$$\n\nApplying the difference of squares formula with $a=1, b=\\sqrt 2$ to simplify the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-\\sqrt{2}}{(1+\\sqrt{2})(1-\\sqrt{2})} & = \\\\ \\frac{1-\\sqrt{2}}{{1}^{2}-(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1-\\sqrt{2}}{1-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{1-\\sqrt{2}}{-1} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186055965 1064 "Let's remind ourselves of the first few powers of $\\textrm{i}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\textrm{i}^{1} &= \\textrm{i} \\\\\n\\textrm{i}^{2} &= -1 \\\\\n\\textrm{i}^{3} &= -\\textrm{i} \\\\\n\\textrm{i}^{4} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the fourth power, we return to the value of $1$, which can also be written as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^0 = 1\n$$\n\nThis means that if we continue to increase the powers, we would cycle through the same four values once again, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5e/5e3f3a42dd591daf109e474eec99e446706e0d8e46f1252540839cd50d957463.png)\n\nSo, for $\\textrm{i}^5$ through $\\textrm{i}^8$, the exact same pattern repeats. Remembering that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^4 = 1\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\textrm{i}^5&=\\textrm{i}^4 \\cdot \\textrm{i} &= \\textrm{i} \\\\[5pt] \\textrm{i}^6&=\\textrm{i}^4 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^2 &=-1 \\\\[5pt] \\textrm{i}^7&=\\textrm{i}^4 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^3 &= -\\textrm{i} \\\\[5pt] \\textrm{i}^8&=\\textrm{i}^4 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^4 &= 1. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for $\\textrm{i}^9$ through to $\\textrm{i}^{12}$, the pattern repeats again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\textrm{i}^9&=(\\textrm{i}^4)^2 \\cdot \\textrm{i} &= \\textrm{i} \\\\[5pt] \\textrm{i}^{10}&=(\\textrm{i}^4)^2 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^2 &=-1 \\\\[5pt] \\textrm{i}^{11}&=(\\textrm{i}^4)^2 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^3 &= -\\textrm{i} \\\\[5pt] \\textrm{i}^{12}&=(\\textrm{i}^4)^2 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^4 &= 1 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to simplify a higher integer power of $\\textrm{i}$, we *divide the power by $4$ and raise $\\textrm{i}$ to the remainder*.\n\nLet's see this idea in action." 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186055965 1064 "Let's remind ourselves of the first few powers of $\\textrm{i}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{i}}^{1} & =\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{3} & =-\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the fourth power, we return to the value of $1,$ which can also be written as $\\textrm{i}^0 = 1.$ This means that if we continue to increase the powers, we would cycle through the same four values once again, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5e/5e3f3a42dd591daf109e474eec99e446706e0d8e46f1252540839cd50d957463.png)\n\nSo, for $\\textrm{i}^5$ through $\\textrm{i}^8,$ the exact same pattern repeats. Remembering that $\\textrm{i}^4 = 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^5&=\\textrm{i}^4 \\cdot \\textrm{i} &= \\textrm{i}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^6&=\\textrm{i}^4 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^2 &=-1\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^7&=\\textrm{i}^4 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^3 &= -\\textrm{i} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^8&=\\textrm{i}^4 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^4 &= 1 .\n\t}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for $\\textrm{i}^9$ through to $\\textrm{i}^{12},$ the pattern repeats again:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^9&=(\\textrm{i}^4)^2 \\cdot \\textrm{i} &= \\textrm{i}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^{10}&=(\\textrm{i}^4)^2 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^2 &=-1\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^{11}&=(\\textrm{i}^4)^2 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^3 &= -\\textrm{i} \\[5pt]\n\t\