=4\\cdot {e}^{0.25t}$ crocodiles per year, where $t$ represents the number of years since the year $2000.$  \n\nWhat was the rate of change of the crocodile population in $2012?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186064839 1074 "The rate of change in the crocodile population $t$ years since $2000$ is equal to the birth rate minus the death rate, that is\n\n$$\nB(t)-D(t).\n$$\n\nSince $2012$ is $12$ years after $2000$, we have $t=12.$ Substituting $t=12,$ the rate of change of the crocodile population in the river  in $2012$ was\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(12)-D(12) & =6\\cdot {e}^{0.25(12)}-4\\cdot {e}^{0.25(12)} \\\\  & =6{e}^{3}-4{e}^{3} \\\\  & =2{e}^{3} \\\\  & ≈40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of the crocodile population in the river in $2012$ is approximately $40$ crocodiles per year." 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186064966 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186064966 1071 "$$\n\n$\\int 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186064966 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int2\\cosh xdx$?" 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186064966 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,⇒\\,\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186064971 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186064971 1071 "$$\n\n$\\int 4{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186064971 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int4csch^{2} xdx$?" 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186064971 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)=-{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,⇒\\,\\int {csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int {csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186065392 1064 "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nBy considering the derivatives of the hyperbolic tangent, secant, cosecant, and cotangent, we arrive at the following results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {\\text{\\sech}}^{2}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{tanh}x+C \\\\ \\int \\text{\\sech}x\\operatorname{tanh}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\text{\\sech}x+C \\\\ \\int \\text{\\csch}x\\operatorname{coth}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\text{\\csch}x+C \\\\ \\int {\\text{\\csch}}^{2}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{coth}x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186065392 1064 "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nBy considering the derivatives of the hyperbolic tangent, secant, cosecant, and cotangent, we arrive at the following results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {sech}^{2}⁡x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x+C \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+C \\\\ \\int {csch}^{2}⁡x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186068830 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515538 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186070332 1075 #edb/ref 17592186514933 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186070332 1078 #edb/ref 17592186514933 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186070333 1075 #edb/ref 17592186515261 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186070333 1078 #edb/ref 17592186515261 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186072476 1078 #edb/ref 17592186072479 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186073715 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515519 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186073715 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515597 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186073739 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515552 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186073740 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515529 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186073740 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515610 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186073786 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515588 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186074071 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186074071 1071 "The midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(a, 2b)$ and $(5a, 7b)$\nis$($\n{{field-1}}\n$$,\n{{field-2}}\n$)$." 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186074071 1071 "The midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(a,2b)$ and $(5a,7b)$\n is$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186074071 1074 "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between ${{(p,-3q)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(-p,7q)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{a+5a}{2},\\frac{2b+7b}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{6a}{2},\\frac{9b}{2}) \\\\  & =(3a,\\frac{9b}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186074373 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186074373 1071 "![](images/38/3865ec425991f0d8ceae82f002d0096f2fd2ea56743173e754b15472b7c85031.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\n> The ratio of triangles to hexagons is $1: 1$ because there is $1$ triangle for every $1$ hexagon." 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186074373 1071 "![](images/38/3865ec425991f0d8ceae82f002d0096f2fd2ea56743173e754b15472b7c85031.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of triangles to hexagons is $1:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there is $1$ triangle for every $\\boxed{}$ hexagon." 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186074373 1074 "First, we count the number of triangles. We see that there are $3$ triangles.\n\nThen, we count the number of hexagons. We see that there are $3$ hexagons.\n\nSo the ratio of triangles to hexagons is $3:3.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,3 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1:1$ is equivalent to $3:3.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of triangles to hexagons is $1:1$ because there is $1$ triangle for every $1$ hexagon.\"" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186089519 1078 #edb/ref 17592186089523 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186092314 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515539 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186092314 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515587 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186092314 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515595 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186092315 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515541 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186092316 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515554 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186092316 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515560 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186092558 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186092558 1071 "A company manufactures electronic components. Of the components manufactured, $45%$ are produced by the first machine, $25%$ are produced by the second machine, and the third machine produces the remaining.\nIf $8%$ of the components produced by the first machine, $4%$ produced by the second machine, and $5%$ produced by the third machine are defective, what is the probability that a randomly selected component is defective?" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186092558 1071 "A company manufactures electronic components. Of the components manufactured, $45%$ are produced by the first machine, $25%$ are produced by the second machine, and the third machine produces the remaining.\nIf $8%$ of the components produced by the first machine, $4%$ produced by the second machine, and $5%$ produced by the third machine are defective, what is the probability that a randomly selected component is defective?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186092558 1074 "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly selected component is defective, and \n\n- let ${B}_{1}$ be the event that the component was produced by the first machine,\n\n- let ${B}_{2}$ be the event that the component was produced by the second machine,\n\n- let ${B}_{3}$ be the event that the component was produced by the third machine.\n\nThen the disjoint union ${B}_{1}⊔{B}_{2}⊔{B}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap {B}_{1})+P(D\\cap {B}_{2})+P(D\\cap {B}_{3}) \\\\  & =P(D|{B}_{1})P({B}_{1})+P(D|{B}_{2})P({B}_{2})+P(D|{B}_{3})P({B}_{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since the first machine produces $45%$ of the components, the second machine produces $25%$ of the components, the third machine produces the remaining\n\n$$\n100%-(45%+25%)=30%.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nP({B}_{1})=0.45,\\,P({B}_{2})=0.25,\\,P({B}_{3})=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $8%$ of the components produced by the first machine, $4%$ produced by the second machine, and $5%$ produced by the third machine are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|{B}_{1})=0.08,\\,P(D|{B}_{2})=0.04,\\,P(D|{B}_{3})=0.05.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected component is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|{B}_{1})P({B}_{1})+P(D|{B}_{2})P({B}_{2})+P(D|{B}_{3})P({B}_{3}) \\\\  & =(0.08)(0.45)+(0.04)(0.25)+(0.05)(0.3) \\\\  & =0.036+0.01+0.015 \\\\  & =0.061.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186114685 1075 #edb/ref 17592186114686 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186114685 1075 #edb/ref 17592186114687 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186114685 1075 #edb/ref 17592186114688 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186114685 1075 #edb/ref 17592186114689 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186114685 1075 #edb/ref 17592186114690 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186114685 1075 #edb/ref 17592186514932 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186114685 1075 #edb/ref 17592186514995 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186114685 1075 #edb/ref 17592186515141 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186114685 1075 #edb/ref 17592186515262 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186114685 1075 #edb/ref 17592186515301 13194139535549 17592186045417 t