\\frac{3}{4}$, find $E[4X + 6Y]$." 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186188538 1074 "We're given $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we first need to compute $\\text{E}[Y].$\n\nThe marginal density function for $Y$ when $0\\le y\\le 2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}y[{x}^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}y({1}^{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the  expected value of $Y$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot \\frac{y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[{y}^{3}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(8-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[4X+6Y] & =4\\,\\text{E}[X]+6\\,\\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =4(\\frac{3}{4})+6(\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =3+8 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186191050 1078 #edb/ref 17592186191053 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186192795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515557 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186192795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515592 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186192796 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515551 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186192796 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515572 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186193194 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186193194 1071 "Suppose $X_{1}, X_{2}, X_{3}, X_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_{i}∼Po(θ)$ with unknown rate parameter $θ$. You're given the particular sample\n$x_{1} = 4, x_{2} = 2, x_{3} = 1, x_{4} = 2$.\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n$l(θ) = aθ + b\\ln θ - \\ln c$.\n\nFind the value of $a + b + c$.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Poisson distribution is given by*\n$f(x) = \\frac{θ^{x}e^{- θ}}{x!}, x = 0, 1, 2, …$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186193194 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ with unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=4,\\,{x}_{2}=2,\\,{x}_{3}=1,\\,{x}_{4}=2.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\theta +b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡c.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Poisson distribution is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\n$$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186193194 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{x}}{x!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}}{{x}_{i}!}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta })+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-n\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}!)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}! & =4!\\cdot 2!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 2!=24\\cdot 2\\cdot 1\\cdot 2=96, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =4+2+1+2=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-4\\theta +9\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡96.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=-4+9+96=101.\n$$" 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186231170 1075 #edb/ref 17592186231171 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186231170 1075 #edb/ref 17592186231172 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186231170 1075 #edb/ref 17592186231173 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186231170 1075 #edb/ref 17592186231174 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186231170 1075 #edb/ref 17592186231175 13194139535549 17592186045417 false] [17592186231170 1075 #edb/ref 17592186514975 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186231170 1075 #edb/ref 17592186515093 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186231170 1075 #edb/ref 17592186515220 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186231170 1075 #edb/ref 17592186515384 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186231170 1075 #edb/ref 17592186515385 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186231170 1078 #edb/ref 17592186514975 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186231464 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515614 13194139535549 17592186045417 true] [17592186231465 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515